From 9dcd9c53d2e7dce4d83994dd7da26bb4d0138cc6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Keywan Ghadami <keywan.ghadami@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 08:23:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Rank an expectation by whether the grammar required it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A message named the whitespace skip instead of the token that would fix
the input. `WS = (WSE | COMMENT)*` - the documented way to support
comments - fails with two expectations, which `merge` promotes to
PRIO_AGGREGATED; a real expectation usually has one, so it lost the
comparison and was *discarded*. Every syntax error in a grammar with
comments read `expected one of: "//", whitespace`.

It is not about comments. `xs:item* "."` over `1 2 x` reported `expected
one of: "#", integer literal` and dropped the `.`, with no custom WS
anywhere: any rule with two or more alternatives suppressed every
single-expectation error at its position.

Two axes, because one is not enough:

* A **requirement** outranks an **optional continuation**. The runtime
  already draws that line - `repeat_recording_bounded` returns the
  element's error below the minimum and merely records it at or above,
  and `opt_recording` only ever records - so `ParseContext::record` is by
  construction the optional path and marks what it stores. That fixes the
  `.`, which is required where another item is not.

* Among optional continuations, the **implicit whitespace skip** ranks
  last. The first axis cannot separate it: in a grammar whose entry rule
  is a repetition (`program = item*`) everything is an optional
  continuation. The code generator now calls the skip through
  `rt::skip_trivia`, so what the skip records is marked as such - no rule
  names, no declaration, and a `WS` a grammar calls itself stays an
  ordinary rule.

Nothing is dropped by either axis: what loses becomes a `note: also
possible here: …` under the message. A repetition's reason for stopping
is often the useful half, so suppressing it was never an option.

Whitespace alone is left out of that note, and that is the criterion the
rest follows from: the skip is greedy, so at this offset it has already
taken everything there was, and supplying more only moves the same
failure to offset + n. "Expected whitespace" asks for an edit that cannot
work. A comment form stays, because a `/` where `//` belongs is a real
mistake.

`tests/expectation_ranking_test.rs` covers all three. The full suite
passes unchanged; `context_reuse_test` caught the first attempt throwing
away the rule stack that carries `in item 4`, so the absorbed error's
stack is kept exactly as `merge` always kept it.
